6. Improving measurement accuracy
One important benchmarking improvement is to generate lookup keys before starting the timer. Otherwise, the time spent formatting medicine codes is included in the lookup measurement.

Replace the lookupTest() method in the program with this version:


java
static double lookupTest(
        Map<String, Medicine> map,
        int n) {

    // Prepare keys outside the timed section
    String[] lookupKeys = new String[LOOKUPS];

    for (int i = 0; i < LOOKUPS; i++) {
        lookupKeys[i] = code(i % n);
    }

    return measure(() -> {

        long sum = 0;

        for (String key : lookupKeys) {

            Medicine m = map.get(key);

            if (m != null)
                sum += m.stock;
        }

        resultHolder = sum;
    });
}
This isolates the map lookup operation more accurately.

7. Scalability inquiry – Acceptable response time
For a hospital pharmacy, I would define the following engineering target:

Performance target
Average lookup response time: less than 1 millisecond per medicine lookup.

For 100,000 lookups, this corresponds to a maximum aggregate lookup time of 100 seconds if performed sequentially at that average limit.

In practice, a production system should also measure the 95th and 99th percentile response times, not just the average.

