LinkedHashMap maintains extra links to preserve insertion order, introducing additional memory overhead.

Deployment decision: For a pharmacy that needs both code-order and insertion-order reports, I would choose LinkedHashMap if preserving insertion order is important to normal system operation, while generating the code-order report by sorting its keys.

However, if fast lookups and sorted reporting are the dominant requirements, HashMap with a separate insertion-order list is also a reasonable choice. TreeMap is preferable when continuously sorted access is more important than maximum lookup speed.

8. Why Hashtable is not preferred in new Java code
Hashtable is an older Java collection class.

Reasons to avoid it in new applications:

Its methods are synchronized, which can introduce unnecessary contention.

It does not allow null keys or null values.

HashMap and LinkedHashMap provide more flexible alternatives.

ConcurrentHashMap is generally a better choice for concurrent key-value access when thread-safe operations are required.

For a modern pharmacy inventory application, HashMap, TreeMap or LinkedHashMap is generally preferable depending on the requirements.

9. Alternative concept – Sorted array with Arrays.binarySearch()
Instead of using a Map, the pharmacy can store medicines in an array sorted by medicine code.

Java provides Arrays.binarySearch() to locate a medicine efficiently.

Java implementation

java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;

class MedicineArray {

    static class Medicine {
        String code;
        String name;
        int stock;

        Medicine(String code, String name, int stock) {
            this.code = code;
            this.name = name;
            this.stock = stock;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Medicine[] medicines = {
            new Medicine("MED003", "Paracetamol", 100),
            new Medicine("MED001", "Amoxicillin", 50),
            new Medicine("MED002", "Cetirizine", 75)
        };

        // Sort medicines by code
        Arrays.sort(
            medicines,
            Comparator.comparing(m -> m.code)
        );

        // Search using binary search
        String searchCode = "MED002";

        int index = Arrays.binarySearch(
            medicines,
            new Medicine(searchCode, "", 0),
            Comparator.comparing(m -> m.code)
        );

        if (index >= 0) {
            System.out.println(
                "Medicine found: "
                + medicines[index].name
            );

            System.out.println(
                "Stock: " + medicines[index].stock
            );
        } else {
            System.out.println("Medicine not found");
        }

        // Print code-order report
        System.out.println("\nMedicine Code Report:");

        for (Medicine m : medicines) {
            System.out.println(m.code + " " + m.name);
        }
    }
}
